{
 "id": "l02",
 "group": "lecture",
 "num": 2,
 "title": "L2 · Atoms & Molecules",
 "subtitle": "Atoms, ions, bonds and chemical reactions",
 "lectures": "Lecture 2",
 "voice": "af_heart",
 "scenes": [
  {
   "lec": "L2",
   "title": "Why chemistry?",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Chemistry",
    "Cells",
    "Body function"
   ],
   "start": 0.4,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Body functions depend on cell functions.",
     "start": 0.4,
     "end": 2.84
    },
    {
     "text": "Cell functions come from chemical changes.",
     "start": 3.29,
     "end": 5.9
    },
    {
     "text": "So A and P starts with chemistry.",
     "start": 6.35,
     "end": 8.15
    }
   ],
   "end": 9.25
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Matter",
   "title": "Matter, elements, atoms",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Matter",
    "Element",
    "Atom"
   ],
   "start": 9.25,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Matter** is anything with mass that takes up space.",
     "start": 9.25,
     "end": 11.9
    },
    {
     "text": "An **element** cannot be broken into simpler substances.",
     "start": 12.35,
     "end": 15.51
    },
    {
     "text": "An **atom** is the smallest unit of an element that keeps its properties.",
     "start": 15.96,
     "end": 19.82
    },
    {
     "text": "Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen are the big four.",
     "start": 20.27,
     "end": 24.12
    },
    {
     "text": "Together they make up 96 percent of the body.",
     "start": 24.57,
     "end": 27.37
    }
   ],
   "end": 28.47
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Atoms",
   "title": "Inside an atom",
   "img": "L2_01.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Proton",
    "Neutron",
    "Electron",
    "Nucleus"
   ],
   "start": 28.47,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The **nucleus** is the atom's core.",
     "start": 28.47,
     "end": 30.32
    },
    {
     "text": "It holds **protons**, which are positive, and **neutrons**, which are neutral.",
     "start": 30.77,
     "end": 34.72
    },
    {
     "text": "**Electrons** are negative. They circle in shells around the nucleus.",
     "start": 35.17,
     "end": 38.97
    },
    {
     "text": "A neutral atom has equal protons and electrons.",
     "start": 39.42,
     "end": 42.52
    }
   ],
   "end": 43.62
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Atoms",
   "title": "Atomic number and mass",
   "img": "L2_03.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Atomic number",
    "Atomic mass"
   ],
   "start": 43.62,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Atomic number** is the number of protons.",
     "start": 43.62,
     "end": 46.13
    },
    {
     "text": "**Atomic mass** is protons plus neutrons.",
     "start": 46.58,
     "end": 49.12
    },
    {
     "text": "Sodium has atomic number 11 and atomic mass 23.",
     "start": 49.57,
     "end": 53.48
    },
    {
     "text": "So sodium has 12 neutrons.",
     "start": 53.93,
     "end": 55.86
    },
    {
     "text": "Neutrons equal atomic mass minus atomic number.",
     "start": 56.31,
     "end": 59.9
    }
   ],
   "end": 61.0
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Shells",
   "title": "Shells and valence",
   "img": "L2_02.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Shells",
    "Valence shell",
    "Valence electrons"
   ],
   "start": 61.0,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The first shell holds 2 electrons. The second holds 8.",
     "start": 61.0,
     "end": 64.52
    },
    {
     "text": "Sodium has 11 electrons: 2, then 8, then 1.",
     "start": 64.97,
     "end": 68.65
    },
    {
     "text": "The outermost shell is the **valence shell**.",
     "start": 69.1,
     "end": 71.13
    },
    {
     "text": "The electrons in it are **valence electrons**.",
     "start": 71.58,
     "end": 74.06
    },
    {
     "text": "Valence electrons decide how an atom bonds.",
     "start": 74.51,
     "end": 77.43
    }
   ],
   "end": 78.53
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Ions",
   "title": "Ions",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Ion",
    "Cation",
    "Anion"
   ],
   "start": 78.53,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "An **ion** is an atom with a charge.",
     "start": 78.53,
     "end": 80.58
    },
    {
     "text": "A **cation** lost electrons. It is positive.",
     "start": 81.03,
     "end": 83.85
    },
    {
     "text": "An **anion** gained electrons. It is negative.",
     "start": 84.3,
     "end": 87.23
    },
    {
     "text": "Sodium loses one electron and becomes a cation.",
     "start": 87.68,
     "end": 90.89
    },
    {
     "text": "Chlorine gains one and becomes an anion, chloride.",
     "start": 91.34,
     "end": 94.45
    }
   ],
   "end": 95.55
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Bonds",
   "title": "Ionic bonds",
   "img": "L2_06.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Ionic bond",
    "NaCl"
   ],
   "start": 95.55,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "An **ionic bond** joins a cation and an anion.",
     "start": 95.55,
     "end": 98.43
    },
    {
     "text": "One atom gives electrons. The other receives them.",
     "start": 98.88,
     "end": 101.94
    },
    {
     "text": "Table salt, **NaCl**, is the example.",
     "start": 102.39,
     "end": 105.32
    },
    {
     "text": "Ionic bonds are weak. They come apart easily in water.",
     "start": 105.77,
     "end": 109.25
    }
   ],
   "end": 110.35
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Bonds",
   "title": "Covalent bonds",
   "img": "L2_07.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Covalent",
    "Polar",
    "Nonpolar"
   ],
   "start": 110.35,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "A **covalent bond** shares electrons. It is strong.",
     "start": 110.35,
     "end": 113.58
    },
    {
     "text": "In a **polar** covalent bond, sharing is unequal. Water is polar.",
     "start": 114.03,
     "end": 118.21
    },
    {
     "text": "In a **nonpolar** covalent bond, sharing is equal. CO₂ is nonpolar.",
     "start": 118.66,
     "end": 124.27
    }
   ],
   "end": 125.37
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Bonds",
   "title": "Hydrogen bonds",
   "img": "L2_09.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Hydrogen bond",
    "Water",
    "DNA"
   ],
   "start": 125.37,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Hydrogen bonds** form between hydrogen atoms of different molecules.",
     "start": 125.37,
     "end": 129.63
    },
    {
     "text": "They are weak.",
     "start": 130.08,
     "end": 130.93
    },
    {
     "text": "They hold water molecules to each other.",
     "start": 131.38,
     "end": 133.32
    },
    {
     "text": "They also hold the bases of **DNA** and RNA together.",
     "start": 133.77,
     "end": 137.35
    }
   ],
   "end": 138.45
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Molecules",
   "title": "Molecules and compounds",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Molecule",
    "Compound",
    "Formula"
   ],
   "start": 138.45,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "On the slides, a **molecule** has one type of atom, like H₂.",
     "start": 138.45,
     "end": 142.27
    },
    {
     "text": "A **compound** has two or more different elements, like CH₄.",
     "start": 142.72,
     "end": 146.17
    },
    {
     "text": "A chemical **formula** lists the atoms. Glucose is C₆H₁₂O₆.",
     "start": 146.62,
     "end": 151.63
    }
   ],
   "end": 152.73
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Energy",
   "title": "Kinetic and potential",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Kinetic",
    "Potential"
   ],
   "start": 152.73,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Energy is the ability to do work.",
     "start": 152.73,
     "end": 154.92
    },
    {
     "text": "**Kinetic** energy is energy of motion: heat, light, movement.",
     "start": 155.37,
     "end": 159.32
    },
    {
     "text": "**Potential** energy is stored, like in a battery or a chemical bond.",
     "start": 159.77,
     "end": 163.85
    }
   ],
   "end": 164.95
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Reactions",
   "title": "Build and break",
   "img": "L2_10.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Synthesis",
    "Decomposition",
    "Exchange"
   ],
   "start": 164.95,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Synthesis** builds. A plus B makes A B. It is anabolic and needs energy.",
     "start": 164.95,
     "end": 169.85
    },
    {
     "text": "**Decomposition** breaks. A B makes A plus B. It is catabolic and releases energy.",
     "start": 170.3,
     "end": 175.72
    },
    {
     "text": "**Exchange** swaps partners. A B plus C D makes A C plus B D.",
     "start": 176.17,
     "end": 180.72
    },
    {
     "text": "Most reactions are reversible, even if it takes years.",
     "start": 181.17,
     "end": 184.53
    }
   ],
   "end": 185.63
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Reactions",
   "title": "Water in, water out",
   "img": "L2_11.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Dehydration synthesis",
    "Hydrolysis"
   ],
   "start": 185.63,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Dehydration synthesis** makes a bond by removing water.",
     "start": 185.63,
     "end": 189.28
    },
    {
     "text": "**Hydrolysis** breaks a bond by adding water.",
     "start": 189.73,
     "end": 192.35
    },
    {
     "text": "Hydro means water. Lysis means break.",
     "start": 192.8,
     "end": 195.49
    }
   ],
   "end": 196.59
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Redox",
   "title": "LEO the lion goes GER",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Oxidation",
    "Reduction"
   ],
   "start": 196.59,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Losing electrons is **oxidation**.",
     "start": 196.59,
     "end": 198.75
    },
    {
     "text": "Gaining electrons is **reduction**.",
     "start": 199.2,
     "end": 201.09
    },
    {
     "text": "They always happen together.",
     "start": 201.54,
     "end": 203.02
    },
    {
     "text": "LEO the lion goes GER. Lose electrons, oxidation. Gain electrons, reduction.",
     "start": 203.47,
     "end": 209.85
    },
    {
     "text": "Sodium is oxidized. Chlorine is reduced.",
     "start": 210.3,
     "end": 213.33
    }
   ],
   "end": 214.43
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Rate",
   "title": "Speeding up reactions",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Temperature",
    "Concentration",
    "Particle size",
    "Catalyst"
   ],
   "start": 214.43,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Four things speed up a reaction.",
     "start": 214.43,
     "end": 216.24
    },
    {
     "text": "Higher **temperature**. Higher **concentration**.",
     "start": 216.69,
     "end": 219.17
    },
    {
     "text": "Smaller **particle size**.",
     "start": 219.62,
     "end": 221.08
    },
    {
     "text": "And a **catalyst**, which lowers the energy needed to react.",
     "start": 221.53,
     "end": 224.82
    },
    {
     "text": "Enzymes are the body's catalysts. You'll meet them in Lecture 3.",
     "start": 225.27,
     "end": 229.13
    }
   ],
   "end": 230.23
  }
 ],
 "duration": 230.23,
 "audio": "stories/l02.mp3"
}